* SKY130 bandgap reference -- CURRENT-MODE (Banba) core + the same folded-cascode error amplifier.
*
* WHY THIS DECK EXISTS. The reference design this benchmark is formulated from -- Hoang et al. /
* Nguyen et al., Spectre library `quocthang`, cell `BGR_modified_3` -- is current-mode Banba-style:
* three matched PMOS legs drive V_X, V_Y and V_REF, the amplifier forces V_X = V_Y, and the summed
* CTAT+PTAT current is delivered into a separate output resistor:
*
*       V_REF = R_ref * ( V_EB / R_CTAT  +  dV_EB / R_PTAT )
*
* Our first SKY130 deck (bgr_sky130.cir.tmpl) is voltage-mode KUIJK instead. That choice had a
* documented cost: it pins V_REF near the bandgap voltage (1.2199 V measured), so TWO of the six
* published 65-nm specifications could not be transplanted -- the V_REF window of 798-802 mV was
* unreachable in that topology and had to be re-anchored. This deck removes that gap. With a free
* output resistor V_REF becomes a design choice, so the PUBLISHED window is usable as published.
*
* This is therefore not an additional topology tried for its own sake; it is the topology the
* reference design actually uses, and it makes the benchmark faithful to its own source.
*
* THE CORE TRIM POINT, derived rather than fitted:
*   zero first-order TC needs  R_PTAT/R_CTAT = -(d dV_EB/dT)/(d V_EB/dT)
*                            = (ln(N) k/q) / 1.7 mV/K = 0.1792/1.7 = 0.105
*   the bandgap sum is then   V_EB + dV_EB * R_CTAT/R_PTAT = 0.7325 + 0.510 = 1.2425 V
*     (V_EB = 0.7325 V is not assumed: it is what the Kuijk deck measures at this PNP and this
*      branch current, since VREF = V_EB + 9.065*V_T*ln(8) = 1.2199 V there)
*   so 800 mV needs           R_ref/R_CTAT = 0.800/1.2425 = 0.644
* The reference netlist uses R_ref/R_CTAT = 36.5/55.3 = 0.660. The two agree to 2.5 %, which is
* the check that the derivation is right rather than merely arithmetically consistent.
*
* Branch current is set ONLY by the core: the loop forces nA = nB, hence
*       I_Q = V_T*ln(N)/R_PTAT      and      V_REF = R_ref*(V_EB/R_CTAT + I_Q)
* independent of every amplifier variable. So V_REF stays at its trim point under optimization and
* the V_REF constraint measures loop-gain-limited error and mirror mismatch, exactly as in the
* Kuijk deck -- the two cases stay comparable.
*
* THE SEVEN DESIGN VARIABLES are the amplifier plus the core mirror, matching the Kuijk Base case
* in count and in kind. `w_pass` there becomes `w_core` here: the Kuijk pass device and the Banba
* mirror leg occupy the same position in the loop -- the single element the amplifier output drives.
*
* IDEAL ELEMENTS, disclosed for the same reasons as in the Kuijk deck: the tail current, the fold
* sinks and the cascode bias are ideal sources, and the resistors are ideal (no PDK resistor model,
* hence no resistor temperature coefficient and no mismatch). In a current-mode core the resistor
* RATIOS set both TC and V_REF, and ratios of like resistors track over temperature, so the
* first-order behaviour survives; the absolute value sets the branch current, so a real resistor TC
* would make power and PSRR drift with temperature in a way this deck does not capture.
*
* LOOP PROBE -- ONE valid cut, unlike the Kuijk deck. Kuijk offers two single-wire chokepoints (the
* pass gate and the pass drain), so probe agreement there is real evidence. In Banba the only
* single-wire chokepoint is the amplifier output driving the three mirror gates: the sense side is
* differential (nA and nB both feed the input pair) and the V_REF leg is a replica OUTSIDE the loop,
* so cutting either would return a complete and plausible WRONG answer. Vz2/Iz2 are therefore a
* second cut on the SAME wire; they exist so that the harness's probe-disarming sequence still
* applies cleanly, and agreement between them is trivial here rather than evidence. Stated so that
* nobody later reads it as a second independent audit.
*
* Node names match the Kuijk deck exactly (vref, nA, nB, nout, nout_b), so spice_problem.py drives
* this deck with no change to the measurement path.

.title SKY130 Banba current-mode bandgap -- folded-cascode error amplifier
* ${CORNER} is 'tt' for every OPTIMIZATION run; the placeholder exists so the PVT pass can
* re-simulate the SAME deck at ss/ff/sf/fs without a second copy of the netlist.
.lib ${SKY130_LIB} ${CORNER}

* ===== the seven design variables (amplifier + core mirror; the core stays at its trim point) =====
.param w_pdiff=${W_PDIFF}      $ input pair XM1/XM2 width, um
.param w_ncasc=${W_NCASC}      $ NMOS cascode XM5/XM6 width, um
.param w_pmir=${W_PMIR}        $ amplifier top PMOS mirror XM7/XM8 width, um
.param w_core=${W_CORE}        $ core current-mirror leg width XMC1/XMC2/XMC3, um
.param l_all=${L_ALL}          $ common channel length, um
.param iss=${ISS}              $ tail current, uA
.param vbnv=${VBNV}            $ NMOS cascode bias, V

* ===== fixed current-mode core, at the derived trim point =====
* N = 8 is the standard emitter ratio, as in the Kuijk deck.
* R_PTAT sets the branch current: I_Q = V_T*ln(8)/R_PTAT = 53.77 mV / 24.8 k = 2.17 uA.
*
* MEASURED TRIM, replacing the analytic estimate. The derivation gave R_PTAT/R_CTAT = 0.105, i.e.
* R_PTAT = 26.2 k; simulated, that is CTAT-heavy (V_REF falls 801.6 -> 791.2 mV over -40..125 C) and
* gives TC = 78 ppm/C. Sweeping R_PTAT locates the turning point between PTAT-dominant and
* CTAT-dominant behaviour at 24.8 k:
*     24.2 k  42.4 ppm (rises)      25.0 k  21.2 ppm (falls)
*     24.6 k  25.2 ppm (rises)      25.2 k  28.7 ppm (falls)
*     24.8 k  18.5 ppm  <- flattest
* The analytic estimate was 5.6 % high because it assumed dV_EB/dT = -1.7 mV/K, which is a textbook
* figure rather than this PNP at this current density. Recorded because the estimate is what makes
* the trim reproducible, and its error is what makes the measurement necessary.
*
* R_ref then sets the output level: 159.90 k puts V_REF at 800.47 mV, inside the PUBLISHED
* 798-802 mV window -- the specification the Kuijk deck could not reach at all.
* Measured at this trim: V_REF 800.47 mV, TC 18.36 ppm/C (Kuijk: 1219.90 mV, 24.49 ppm/C).
* Declared LOWERCASE and one per line, matching the design variables. That is not cosmetic:
* `alterparam` is silently accepted on a name ngspice does not have, so a mismatch changes nothing
* and reports nothing. A first attempt to trim these with UPPERCASE names on a single .param line
* returned byte-identical metrics for seven different R_ptat values -- the tell was that the sweep
* was too consistent, not that anything failed.
.param n_pnp=8
.param r_ptat=24.8k
.param r_ctat=248.5k
.param r_ref=159.90k
* fold sinks must exceed iss/2 or the fold starves; tie them structurally so that a starved fold --
* the dominant non-convergence mode -- cannot be proposed at all.
.param ib='0.8*iss'

* AC 1 V on the rail makes |v(vref)| the supply-to-output transfer directly.
Vdd vdd 0 DC ${VDD_DC} AC 1

* ---- core: three matched PMOS legs, gates driven by the amplifier output ----
* The gate net is nout_c, the far side of BOTH loop probes, so arming Vz cuts every path
* from the amplifier output to the core.
XMC1 nA   nout_c vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_core} L={l_all}
XMC2 nB   nout_c vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_core} L={l_all}
XMC3 vref nout_c vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_core} L={l_all}

* ---- branch A: unit PNP, diode-connected, in parallel with R_CTAT ----
XQ1 0 0 nA sky130_fd_pr__pnp_05v5_W3p40L3p40
Rca nA 0 {r_ctat}

* ---- branch B: R_PTAT in series with the N-times PNP, in parallel with R_CTAT ----
Rpt nB nC {r_ptat}
XQ2 0 0 nC sky130_fd_pr__pnp_05v5_W3p40L3p40 m={n_pnp}
Rcb nB 0 {r_ctat}

* ---- output leg: the summed CTAT+PTAT current into the free output resistor ----
Rrf vref 0 {r_ref}

* ---- folded-cascode error amplifier, PMOS input pair ----
* Connections are IDENTICAL to the Kuijk deck, and that is a result rather than a convenience.
* Branch B carries R_PTAT in series with its diode, so its incremental resistance is the larger:
* raising the mirror current raises nB faster than nA. Negative feedback therefore needs
* nB -> nout to be non-inverting and nA -> nout inverting, which is what this amplifier already
* provides (nB on XM1, nA on XM2). The Kuijk core has the same asymmetry -- R3 in series in the
* nB branch -- so the polarity carries over unchanged.
Iss  vdd tail dc 'iss*1u'
XM1  fA nB tail vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_pdiff} L={l_all}
XM2  fB nA tail vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_pdiff} L={l_all}
Ib1  fA 0 dc 'ib*1u'
Ib2  fB 0 dc 'ib*1u'
Vbn  vbn 0 dc {vbnv}
XM5  nol  vbn fA 0 sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w_ncasc} L={l_all}
XM6  nout vbn fB 0 sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 W={w_ncasc} L={l_all}
XM7  nol  nol vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_pmir} L={l_all}
XM8  nout nol vdd vdd sky130_fd_pr__pfet_01v8 W={w_pmir} L={l_all}

* ---- start-up leak ----
* The all-off state (mirrors off, nout at vdd, every branch at 0 V) is a valid DC solution of this
* core, so the deck needs a path that makes it unstable. An 80 Meg leak pulls the amplifier output
* down by a few mV at zero current, which turns the mirrors on; at the operating point it draws
* ~10 nA and is negligible in the power budget. Same device and same value as the Kuijk deck, so
* the two cases are not separated by their start-up arrangement.
Rstart nout 0 80meg

* ===== Tian loop-gain probe: node A = nout (amplifier output), node B = nout_b =====
* These two names are an INTERFACE, not a choice: the harness measures mag(v(nout)-v(nout_b))
* with @Vz[acmag]=1 and refuses the candidate unless it is exactly 1. Naming the amplifier
* output anything else puts the production probe on the wrong wire, and the guard then reads
* |dV| = 0 -- which is what it is for.
* Vz with dc 0 is an ideal wire and Iz with dc 0 is an open circuit, so with both acmag = 0 the
* loop stays closed and every other analysis is unperturbed.
*
* The mirror-gate net is the ONLY single-wire chokepoint in this loop. Do NOT cut on the sense side:
* it is differential, so cutting one branch leaves the loop closed through the other and yields a
* complete, plausible, WRONG answer with no error message. Do NOT cut the V_REF leg either: XMC3 is
* a replica outside the loop, so a cut there measures no loop at all.
*
* The explicit "ac 0" is required, not cosmetic: it sets the ac-given flag so that a later
* `alter @Vz[acmag]=1` takes effect. `reset` discards every alter, so the probe must be re-armed
* after each reset.
Vz nout nout_b dc 0 ac 0
Iz 0    nout   dc 0 ac 0

* ===== second cut, on the SAME wire -- present for harness compatibility, NOT an audit =====
* See the header: Banba admits one valid Tian cut, so agreement between these two is trivial. They
* exist because the harness disarms Vz2/Iz2 alongside Vz/Iz before every loop-gain run, and `alter`
* on a non-existent element is an error rather than a no-op.
Vz2 nout_b nout_c dc 0 ac 0
Iz2 0      nout_b dc 0 ac 0

.end
